Ben, the CEO of Resy,
an app I'm involved in,

00:09:35.967 --> 00:09:38.032
had a great discussion
with some thought leaders

00:09:38.032 --> 00:09:39.355
in the restaurant space around this.

00:09:39.355 --> 00:09:40.353
It's very fascinating.

00:09:40.353 --> 00:09:43.055
My, so I think Danny is amazing

00:09:43.055 --> 00:09:45.044
and always innovating and
doing incredible things

00:09:45.044 --> 00:09:46.759
and I think it's amazing
that he's doing something

00:09:46.759 --> 00:09:49.177
that I think really
takes care of his staff

00:09:49.177 --> 00:09:50.950
and his internal culture.

00:09:50.950 --> 00:09:51.846
I think it's interesting.

00:09:51.846 --> 00:09:53.964
You know, I...

00:09:55.284 --> 00:09:57.512
This is actually not my general thought.

00:09:57.512 --> 00:09:59.881
This is my, one thing
I do well in marketing

00:09:59.881 --> 00:10:02.923
is I don't think about what I'm gonna do,

00:10:02.923 --> 00:10:04.850
and then think everybody's gonna do that.

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:06.079
It's been very successful for me.

00:10:06.079 --> 00:10:07.635
I try to think of the general masses.

00:10:07.635 --> 00:10:09.330
I have a vibe for that.

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:11.026
It's not what I thought about Twitter.

00:10:11.026 --> 00:10:12.837
Man, I don't even like social media.

00:10:12.837 --> 00:10:13.997
What do you think about that?

00:10:13.997 --> 00:10:15.181
I don't know, like if I wasn't

00:10:15.181 --> 00:10:16.552
in the marketing and business world,

00:10:16.552 --> 00:10:18.340
I don't think I'd be really using it.

00:10:18.340 --> 00:10:20.267
Like, I never took pictures as a kid.

00:10:20.267 --> 00:10:21.544
Like, there's no, why do you think I have

00:10:21.544 --> 00:10:22.589
no Throwback Thursday pictures?

00:10:22.589 --> 00:10:24.260
There's no freakin' pictures of me

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:26.885
in my life, Mom, and so...

00:10:26.885 --> 00:10:27.744
(mimics camera clicking)

00:10:27.744 --> 00:10:28.974
That's a little inside joke with my mom.

00:10:28.974 --> 00:10:31.983
She made all mental pictures.

00:10:33.363 --> 00:10:36.537
So I think that, I think that,

00:10:38.297 --> 00:10:40.561
I think that I personally
am in a weird place

00:10:40.561 --> 00:10:43.950
because I'm still gonna
tip cash on top of it

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:45.180
because it's just in me.

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:47.155
Like, I was a stock boy
that took out boxes,

00:10:47.155 --> 00:10:48.060
and people gave me tips.

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:49.593
It's so engrained in me.

00:10:49.593 --> 00:10:51.915
So even though I know I'm paying 21% more,

00:10:51.915 --> 00:10:54.631
or whatever it is, I'm
gonna probably put more cash

00:10:54.631 --> 00:10:55.816
just 'cause, and then I'm scared

00:10:55.816 --> 00:10:57.162
that that's gonna break the whole system

00:10:57.162 --> 00:10:59.136
because if people still
arbitrarily do that,

00:10:59.136 --> 00:11:00.065
then what did you do?

00:11:00.065 --> 00:11:01.735
You just raised it 21%,

00:11:01.735 --> 00:11:05.265
but I think the flexibility
that allows organizations,

00:11:05.265 --> 00:11:07.564
the way you can take care
of people that work for you

00:11:07.564 --> 00:11:10.072
which will then, in turn,
create better service.

00:11:10.072 --> 00:11:11.569
I also think that an interesting model

00:11:11.569 --> 00:11:14.251
could have been just raising
the food prices in general.

00:11:14.251 --> 00:11:15.504
I think that's a fascinating thing

00:11:15.504 --> 00:11:17.319
about the restaurant world.

00:11:17.319 --> 00:11:18.872
You know, the truth is
I really don't know.

00:11:18.872 --> 00:11:21.867
I think that, I wonder
for people that are,

00:11:21.867 --> 00:11:24.143
I think the economy's very good right now.

00:11:24.143 --> 00:11:25.652
I think if tomorrow the Wall Street cats

00:11:25.652 --> 00:11:28.462
are up to their no good
and shit hits the fan,

00:11:28.462 --> 00:11:29.436
are people gonna be like, "Well crap,

00:11:29.436 --> 00:11:30.226
"I don't wanna go there

00:11:30.226 --> 00:11:31.712
"'cause I'm paying it 21% vig,

00:11:31.712 --> 00:11:35.922
"and maybe I'm in 10% tip mode right now?"

00:11:35.922 --> 00:11:37.772
How do you tip, Steve?

00:11:37.772 --> 00:11:39.977
- I pretty much 20, just by default.

00:11:39.977 --> 00:11:40.813
- [Gary] D. Rock?

00:11:40.813 --> 00:11:41.862
- [DRock] 25?

00:11:41.862 --> 00:11:42.508
- Really?

00:11:42.508 --> 00:11:44.088
- [DRock] Yeah, I
double the first number,

00:11:44.088 --> 00:11:45.969
then add like a few more.

00:11:45.969 --> 00:11:47.199
- On any kind of bill?

00:11:47.199 --> 00:11:48.499
- [DRock] Pretty much.

00:11:48.499 --> 00:11:51.401
- I'll do like 20% and then
just go up to the next dollar.

00:11:51.401 --> 00:11:53.497
- [Gary] Got it. Staphon?

00:11:53.497 --> 00:11:55.001
(mumbles)

00:11:55.001 --> 00:11:56.621
- There's my man.

00:11:56.621 --> 00:11:57.764
Way to go honest.

00:11:57.764 --> 00:11:59.157
Yeah, I mean, but those
are big numbers, right?

00:11:59.157 --> 00:12:01.617
Like, those aren't 15, which
is a lot of what people,

00:12:01.617 --> 00:12:03.242
older demo pays at 15.

00:12:03.242 --> 00:12:05.837
- [Steve] Yeah.
- It kinda snuck up.

00:12:05.844 --> 00:12:06.448
I don't know.

00:12:06.448 --> 00:12:09.510
I think it's a very intriguing model.

00:12:09.510 --> 00:12:11.184
It's forcing something on the consumer,

00:12:11.184 --> 00:12:12.508
which I think is fascinating.

00:12:12.508 --> 00:12:15.642
I think he has the brand
to get away with it

00:12:15.642 --> 00:12:17.082
for the people that know.

00:12:17.082 --> 00:12:19.762
I think a lot of people won't
even know, won't even realize.

00:12:19.762 --> 00:12:22.237
They'll realize when
there's no line for a tip,

00:12:22.237 --> 00:12:23.489
and I'm curious how their reaction will be

00:12:23.489 --> 00:12:25.162
that they got forced into a tip.

00:12:25.162 --> 00:12:26.531
Some people get antsy when they're forced

00:12:26.531 --> 00:12:28.366
into a tip for six or more.

00:12:28.366 --> 00:12:29.364
So, I don't know.

00:12:29.364 --> 00:12:30.525
I think it's very individual,

00:12:30.525 --> 00:12:32.244
and I think there'll be a lot of positive,

00:12:32.244 --> 00:12:33.730
definitely in the industry,

00:12:33.730 --> 00:12:35.820
and there'll be some negative
from the end consumer.

00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:38.351
For me personally, it's just
gonna make it more expensive

00:12:38.351 --> 00:12:40.602
for me to go to USV places, US,

00:12:40.602 --> 00:12:43.497
you know, those places so

00:12:43.497 --> 00:12:47.103
because I'm going to still tip.

00:12:47.103 --> 00:12:49.510
What are your thoughts, Steve?

00:12:49.510 --> 00:12:50.455
- I don't know.

00:12:50.455 --> 00:12:51.098
- Do you know about it?

00:12:51.098 --> 00:12:52.468
- Oh yeah, no I'm very familiar with it.

00:12:52.468 --> 00:12:53.814
I don't know, like, I came up as an actor,

00:12:53.814 --> 00:12:55.749
so I know tons of people
in the service industry.

00:12:55.749 --> 00:13:00.014
So like, whatever ends up
screwing them the least is

00:13:00.014 --> 00:13:02.405
I think it's a huge positive.

00:13:02.405 --> 00:13:03.820
It's kind of a scary situation

00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:07.485
to be forced to rely on

00:13:07.485 --> 00:13:09.533
what's obviously a
subjective judgment call,

00:13:09.533 --> 00:13:11.461
but when it's established
in order to pay rent--

00:13:11.461 --> 00:13:13.357
- I think the thing that
everybody's gonna worry about is

00:13:13.357 --> 00:13:14.572
does the service change, right?

00:13:14.572 --> 00:13:17.173
Like, do the people
that hustle the most...

00:13:17.173 --> 00:13:17.915
That's a real challenge.

00:13:17.915 --> 00:13:20.004
I think Danny will pull
it off operationally,

00:13:20.004 --> 00:13:21.677
but I think fast followers won't,

00:13:21.677 --> 00:13:24.905
and then you're not allowing
the best servers to win

00:13:24.905 --> 00:13:26.883
which eliminates meritocracy,

00:13:26.883 --> 00:13:30.152
which then creates lowest common courtesy

00:13:30.152 --> 00:13:31.521
and service.

00:13:31.521 --> 00:13:32.473
- [Steve] I mean, we don't
have a tipping structure

00:13:32.473 --> 00:13:34.035
at VaynerMedia.
- I get it.

00:13:34.035 --> 00:13:35.632
- [Steve] We don't have to
worry about, ya know, the--

00:13:35.632 --> 00:13:38.051
- I totally understand.

00:13:38.051 --> 00:13:39.350
I think that's easily handled

00:13:39.350 --> 00:13:40.531
'cause you could just fire, right?

00:13:40.531 --> 00:13:42.342
Like or you could just give raises.

00:13:42.342 --> 00:13:44.199
I mean there's, a lot
of people always deploy

00:13:44.199 --> 00:13:46.161
that while we don't have
tipping in our thing,

00:13:46.161 --> 00:13:48.843
sure, you have others
ways to like, ya know.

00:13:48.843 --> 00:13:50.933
There's levers in all games, right?

00:13:50.933 --> 00:13:52.656
I think when, look I mean,

00:13:52.656 --> 00:13:55.507
this gets into a Unionized conversation.

00:13:55.507 --> 00:13:57.063
I was born in the Soviet Union.

00:13:57.063 --> 00:13:58.990
We've seen that play out at scale.

00:13:58.990 --> 00:14:03.990
It's really hard to
suppress humans, and so...

00:14:04.145 --> 00:14:06.235
But the truth is on a microlevel,

00:14:06.235 --> 00:14:08.561
on a Danny Meyer's establishments level,

00:14:08.561 --> 00:14:10.088
I have enormous confidence
that he can do it.

00:14:10.088 --> 00:14:13.759
I very much feel that I could
do very rogue shit at Vayner

00:14:13.759 --> 00:14:15.197
because it's only 600 people,

00:14:15.197 --> 00:14:17.384
It's only 1000 people,
whatever it's gonna be

00:14:17.384 --> 00:14:18.702
that I could pull those levers.

00:14:18.702 --> 00:14:21.806
At super scale is when
it gets interesting.

00:14:23.266 --> 00:14:25.988
- [Steve] Next question from Dana Gaiser.

00:14:25.988 --> 00:14:26.481
- Dana.

00:14:26.481 --> 00:14:27.666
- Hi, my name is Dana Gaiser.

00:14:27.666 --> 00:14:29.848
I work for Lauber Imports in New York,

00:14:29.848 --> 00:14:33.076
and I am a master sommelier hopeful.

00:14:33.076 --> 00:14:34.294
I wanted to know your opinion

00:14:34.294 --> 00:14:36.901
on the future of the master sommelier

00:14:36.901 --> 00:14:39.670
in reference to

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:42.863
the proliferation of social media,

00:14:42.863 --> 00:14:46.173
bloggers, powerful wine
critics, et cetera.

00:14:46.173 --> 00:14:47.064
- Great question, Dana.

00:14:47.064 --> 00:14:49.677
Dana, I think much of what's
going on in this episode?

00:14:49.677 --> 00:14:51.116
Things have their moments,

00:14:51.116 --> 00:14:52.982
and right now, because of the movie

00:14:52.982 --> 00:14:54.368
and because of this show coming out,

00:14:54.368 --> 00:14:56.131
like people are paying
more attention to somms,

00:14:56.131 --> 00:14:58.153
and I think you'll have more
people get into the game.

00:14:58.153 --> 00:14:59.088
We have way more.

00:14:59.088 --> 00:15:00.497
I run into somms and people

00:15:00.497 --> 00:15:02.842
that are aspiring to be
master somms all the time

00:15:02.842 --> 00:15:05.001
because a lot of them started
watching Wine Library TV

00:15:05.001 --> 00:15:06.348
when they were in high school or college

00:15:06.348 --> 00:15:07.602
which makes me feel very old.

00:15:07.602 --> 00:15:10.219
Getting on the 10 year anniversary
of the show in February

00:15:10.219 --> 00:15:12.091
which is insane.

00:15:12.091 --> 00:15:15.172
And so I think that,

00:15:15.172 --> 00:15:16.193
I think there's gonna be a moment here

00:15:16.193 --> 00:15:17.795
for the next 36 to 60 months

00:15:17.795 --> 00:15:20.257
that it's gonna be cool
and respected more.

00:15:20.257 --> 00:15:23.900
I mean look, if you were a
chef in the 1970s or 80s,

00:15:23.900 --> 00:15:25.944
you were the help in America.

00:15:25.944 --> 00:15:27.083
Now you're a celebrity.

00:15:27.083 --> 00:15:29.241
So I think there's gonna
be an amazing opportunity.

00:15:29.241 --> 00:15:31.596
I think the bloggers and
the social media things

00:15:31.596 --> 00:15:32.586
and things of that nature are just gonna

00:15:32.586 --> 00:15:34.879
amplify the awareness of somms.

00:15:34.879 --> 00:15:37.364
I think somms have a lot of
opportunity to review wines.

00:15:37.364 --> 00:15:38.780
One thing I wanna do, as a matter of fact

00:15:38.780 --> 00:15:39.824
leave in the comments
if you wanna do this.

00:15:39.824 --> 00:15:41.962
I wanna start sending somms
wines from Wine Library

00:15:41.962 --> 00:15:43.447
and have them review on
the hundred point scale

00:15:43.447 --> 00:15:44.539
and using those.

00:15:44.539 --> 00:15:46.304
I think their opinions matter quite a bit,

00:15:46.304 --> 00:15:48.763
and so I'm looking for more
democratization at Wine Library

00:15:48.763 --> 00:15:51.501
on shelf talkers, not
just Parker, Spectator,

00:15:51.501 --> 00:15:53.375
ya know Galloni or me.

00:15:53.375 --> 00:15:55.535
I want like somm of X, Y, Z establishment.

00:15:55.535 --> 00:15:59.114
So I think that's one thing
that I think you'll see more of.

00:15:59.114 --> 00:16:00.922
So I think there's gonna
be a nice half decade here

00:16:00.922 --> 00:16:03.569
of more money for you guys and gals

00:16:03.569 --> 00:16:07.334
for events and private
tastings and, you know.

00:16:07.334 --> 00:16:08.768
When I was doing Wine Library,

00:16:08.768 --> 00:16:10.280
and I was doing private
tastings for people,

00:16:10.280 --> 00:16:11.557
I was the help.

00:16:11.557 --> 00:16:13.582
Like, I was a retail store owner

00:16:13.582 --> 00:16:15.667
that I would come to your
house and I would pour.

00:16:15.667 --> 00:16:17.362
I'm a bartender, right?

00:16:17.362 --> 00:16:19.172
And yes, I gave my thoughts a little bit.

00:16:19.172 --> 00:16:21.309
When I became Wine Library TV Gary,

00:16:21.309 --> 00:16:24.005
people paid me $5000
to come to their house

00:16:24.005 --> 00:16:25.326
and be the star.

00:16:25.326 --> 00:16:26.672
It was just a little bit of a shift

00:16:26.672 --> 00:16:28.135
of exposure and repositioning.

00:16:28.135 --> 00:16:29.250
No different than Bobby Flay

00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:32.291
compared to the best chef
in 1970 in New York City.

00:16:32.291 --> 00:16:34.242
Help versus celebrity.

00:16:34.242 --> 00:16:35.635
I think master somms are about to go

00:16:35.635 --> 00:16:38.264
through a really nice half decade.

00:16:38.264 --> 00:16:42.727
- [Steve] Alright, next
we have Jane Lopes.

00:16:42.727 --> 00:16:44.265
- Jane.

00:16:47.741 --> 00:16:48.754
- Hi, I'm Jane Lopes.

00:16:48.754 --> 00:16:51.263
I'm a sommelier at Eleven Madison Park.

00:16:51.263 --> 00:16:52.848
My question for Gary is

00:16:52.848 --> 00:16:57.285
with all the recent press

00:16:57.285 --> 00:17:01.350
on sommeliers in the last few years,

00:17:01.350 --> 00:17:03.172
what do you think that's
gonna do to the industry

00:17:03.172 --> 00:17:05.897
for consumers, for wine, for restaurants.

00:17:05.897 --> 00:17:08.304
Is it ultimately a good or a bad thing?

00:17:08.304 --> 00:17:09.874
- Kind of similar to the last question.

00:17:09.874 --> 00:17:11.136
Kind of similar to the
last question, Jane.

00:17:11.136 --> 00:17:13.272
I'll just, it is what it
is, the serendipity of it.

00:17:13.272 --> 00:17:15.848
I think it's a good and a bad thing.

00:17:15.848 --> 00:17:17.104
I think it comes down to you, Jane,

00:17:17.104 --> 00:17:17.984
and all the other people.

00:17:17.984 --> 00:17:21.929
With greater power and leverage...

00:17:21.929 --> 00:17:23.983
Money and exposure.

00:17:23.983 --> 00:17:27.412
I do not believe that money
and fame change anybody.

00:17:27.412 --> 00:17:32.172
I think it just exposes who
that person actually is.

00:17:32.172 --> 00:17:34.448
So, do I think more
exposure around you, Jane,

00:17:34.448 --> 00:17:36.747
and all the other, your
cast mates on this show,

00:17:36.747 --> 00:17:38.671
is good, bad, or indifferent
for the end consumer?

00:17:38.671 --> 00:17:41.275
I think it comes down to you guys.

00:17:41.275 --> 00:17:43.294
So Jane, you might be
awesome about it, right?

00:17:43.294 --> 00:17:44.851
You may have a bunch of young people,

00:17:44.851 --> 00:17:46.848
16-year-olds in New York,
going to the kind of place

00:17:46.848 --> 00:17:49.332
that you work at, you know,
may ask you a question

00:17:49.332 --> 00:17:50.515
and look up to you and say,

00:17:50.515 --> 00:17:51.700
"I wanna be like you one day."

00:17:51.700 --> 00:17:52.977
And you could be encouraging,

00:17:52.977 --> 00:17:54.439
or you could be a jerk about it, right?

00:17:54.439 --> 00:17:57.893
Like, it's like, it's just how you play

00:17:57.893 --> 00:18:01.653
this newfound exposure fame leverage,

00:18:01.653 --> 00:18:03.124
people looking at you differently,

00:18:03.124 --> 00:18:05.121
and so if you say that, "Oh my God,

00:18:05.121 --> 00:18:07.449
"now I'm important," and
you become more douchey,

00:18:07.449 --> 00:18:09.951
like that has happened
in so many industries,

00:18:09.951 --> 00:18:12.737
art, music, wine, food,

00:18:12.737 --> 00:18:14.246
well then that's bad for the consumer

00:18:14.246 --> 00:18:15.940
because now we're suppressing people.

00:18:15.940 --> 00:18:19.238
If you take your found leverage

00:18:19.238 --> 00:18:22.350
and you encourage people
and you use it to teach them

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:24.044
about different wines and you get people

00:18:24.044 --> 00:18:26.668
to start drinking chinion
from the Loire Valley

00:18:26.668 --> 00:18:28.942
like my agenda was, or Portugese wines

00:18:28.942 --> 00:18:30.198
or all this amazing thing?

00:18:30.198 --> 00:18:31.660
Well then you're doing a
great thing for the consumer

00:18:31.660 --> 00:18:34.346
because the more different
kinds of wines they taste,

00:18:34.346 --> 00:18:35.816
the more they're gonna
appreciate this incredible thing

00:18:35.816 --> 00:18:37.070
that we're all passionate about.

00:18:37.070 --> 00:18:40.459
So, I think it comes down
to the individual somm.

00:18:40.459 --> 00:18:42.747
And so there's six, right?

00:18:42.747 --> 00:18:45.084
So right, so two of you
may be incredible about it,

00:18:45.084 --> 00:18:46.149
three of you might be average about it,

00:18:46.149 --> 00:18:47.239
one of you might be a jerk about it,

00:18:47.239 --> 00:18:50.816
and then that's what the net
score is for the end consumer.

00:18:50.816 --> 00:18:52.957
- [Steve] Great, last one

00:18:52.957 --> 00:18:54.833
from Josh.

00:18:54.833 --> 00:18:58.269
- So Gary, we need to know

00:18:58.269 --> 00:19:00.939
what wine will Henrik Lundqvist drink

00:19:00.939 --> 00:19:03.679
from the Stanley Cup when
the Rangers win it this year,

00:19:03.679 --> 00:19:06.924
and will I be on hand to
provide proper service?

00:19:06.924 --> 00:19:09.388
- That's an amazing question.

00:19:09.388 --> 00:19:11.024
Josh, great question, brother.

00:19:11.024 --> 00:19:12.780
1994 Rangers winning the cup

00:19:12.780 --> 00:19:13.825
is one of the great days of my life.

